p.incident.damages, capUsedSoFar);\n  capUsedByStepIndex.set(step.policy, capUsedSoFar + claimResult.payout);\n  return claimResult;\n};\n\nconst results = scenario.steps.map((step, index): StepResult =>\n  step.op === \"quote\" ? runQuoteStep(step, index) : runClaimStep(step)\n);\n```\n- Constants: 1 (`0`) = 1\n- Bindings: step, index, premium, policyItems, capUsedSoFar, claimResult = 6\n- Invocations: set, computeQuote, get, get, computeClaim, set, map, runQuoteStep, runClaimStep = 9 → 18\n- Conditionals: 1 (ternary) = 4\n- Loops: 1 (`map`) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\n**Total Mass: 1 + 6 + 18 + 4 + 5 = 34**\n\n## Refactoring Applied\n\n- **Rule 2 (Reveals Intent)**: Extracted `runQuoteStep` and `runClaimStep` from the single anonymous `.map()` callback. Each function name now documents exactly what happens for its step type, and the top-level `.map()` reads as a clean dispatch table (`step.op === \"quote\" ? runQuoteStep(...) : runClaimStep(...)`) instead of an inline branching block.\n- **Mass change**: 30 → 34 (+4). Mass increased slightly due to two extra invocation calls, but per the guidelines **Rule 2 trumps APP** — the extraction meaningfully improves readability by separating two independent concerns that will only grow more distinct as claim-step validation (unknown item types, cap exhaustion, negative damages) is added in upcoming tests.\n- **Rules 3/4**: No duplication found to extract; no unnecessary elements to remove. The two new named functions are justified abstractions, not over-engineering, since `QuoteStep`/`ClaimStep` already exist as distinct types.\n\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: Extracted `runQuoteStep`/`runClaimStep` helper functions to replace the inline `if` branch in `runScenario`'s map callback (Rule 2: Reveals Intent).\n**Mass Change**: 30 -> 34 (+4, accepted per Rule 2 > APP)\n**Tests**: All 36 passing (4 todo, unchanged)"}],"details":{"mode":"single","agentScope":"both","projectAgentsDir":"/home/experimenter/experiments/r